# Test suites

When creating or editing a ScalaTest suite, follow this format.

## Naming

Name the suite after the **single type under test**:

| Location | Template | Example |
|---|---|---|
| `connector` (unit) | `{Type}Spec` | `QueryLayerConfigSpec` |
| `integration` | `{Type}IntegrationSpec` | `GetItemByIdRequestBuilderIntegrationSpec` |

Do not invent other suffixes (`Test`, `Tests`, `IT`, …). Abstract base suites that are not tied to one production type (e.g. `BasicSpec`, `ArcgisIntegrationSpec`) are exempt from this template.

## Inheritance

Every suite — concrete or abstract — must extend [[BasicSpec]] **or** another abstract suite that already extends it (e.g. [[ArcgisIntegrationSpec]], [[RequestBuilderIntegrationSpec]]).

Do not extend `AnyFunSpec` (or any other ScalaTest style) directly.

```scala
// ❌ BAD — skips BasicSpec
class TokenSpec extends AnyFunSpec with Matchers

// ✅ GOOD — unit suite
class QueryLayerConfigSpec extends BasicSpec

// ✅ GOOD — integration suite via a BasicSpec descendant
class GetItemByIdRequestBuilderIntegrationSpec
  extends RequestBuilderIntegrationSpec
```

## Structure

A concrete suite always opens with two nested `describe` blocks:

1. **Subject** — `describe(anInstanceOf[T])` when `T` is a class / case class / trait; `describe(`object`[T.type])` when `T` is a companion (or standalone) object. Use the helpers from [[BasicSpec]]; do not hard-code the title string.
2. **Expectation** — `describe(SHOULD)`, using the `SHOULD` constant from [[BasicSpec]]. Use `describe(SHOULD_NOT)` only for the negative counterpart.

Further `describe` / `it` blocks go **inside** that second describe. The `describe` / `it` strings themselves are not methods and get no Scaladoc.

```scala
// ❌ BAD — hardcoded title, missing SHOULD
class QueryLayerConfigSpec extends BasicSpec {
  describe("QueryLayerConfig") {
    it("extracts where") { ... }
  }
}

// ✅ GOOD — class under test
class QueryLayerConfigSpec extends BasicSpec {
  describe(anInstanceOf[QueryLayerConfig]) {
    describe(SHOULD) {
      it("extract the where clause") { ... }
    }
  }
}

// ✅ GOOD — object under test
class EnumsSpec extends BasicSpec {
  describe(`object`[Enums.type]) {
    describe(SHOULD) {
      it("return the first enum constant that matches a predicate") { ... }
    }
  }
}
```

## Notes

- One production type per suite; put helpers on the suite companion when needed.
- Mix extra traits (`OptionValues`, `JavaMapMixins`, …) **after** the `BasicSpec` (or descendant) parent.
- Concrete suites get no class-level Scaladoc; methods inside them (and on the suite companion) always do (see `scaladoc-classes`, `scaladoc-companions`, and `scaladoc-methods`).
